solution_steps:
Expanding along the first row, we get
\[
\begin{vmatrix}
1 & -3 & 3 \\
0 & 5 & -1 \\
4 & -2 & 1
\end{vmatrix}
= 1 \cdot
\begin{vmatrix}
5 & -1 \\
-2 & 1
\end{vmatrix}
- (-3) \cdot
\begin{vmatrix}
0 & -1 \\
4 & 1
\end{vmatrix}
+ 3 \cdot
\begin{vmatrix}
0 & 5 \\
4 & -2
\end{vmatrix}
\]
Evaluating each determinant, we have
\[
1 \cdot (5 \cdot 1 - (-1) \cdot (-2)) - (-3) \cdot (0 \cdot 1 - (-1) \cdot 4) + 3 \cdot (0 \cdot (-2) - 5 \cdot 4)
= 1 \cdot (5 - 2) - (-3) \cdot (-4) + 3 \cdot (-20)
= 3 + 12 - 60 = \boxed{-45}
\]
The answer is: -45

One way to solve this system of equations is by elimination.
To eliminate $x$, we can multiply the first equation by 5 and the second equation by 2, giving us \begin{align*} 10x - 15y &= -25,\\ 10x - 4y &= 8. \end{align*}
Subtracting the second equation from the first, we get \begin{align*} 10x - 15y - (10x - 4y) &= -25 - 8,\\ -11y &= -33,\\ y &= 3. \end{align*}
Substituting this value of $y$ into the first equation, we can solve for $x$: \begin{align*} 2x - 3(3) &= -5,\\ 2x - 9 &= -5,\\ 2x &= 4,\\ x &= 2. \end{align*}
So the ordered pair $(x,y)$ that satisfies both equations is $\boxed{(2,3)}$.
The answer is: (2,3)

