solution_steps:
To find the decimal representation of $\frac{1}{129}$, we can perform long division:
$$
\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0.00} & 7 & 7 & 5 & 1 & 1 & \ldots \\
\cline{2-9}
129 & 1 & . & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \ldots \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{1} & 2 & 9 \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{10} & 7 & 0 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 1 & 2 & 9 \\
\cline{3-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & 8 & 1 & 0 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & 7 & 7 & 4 \\
\cline{4-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 3 & 3 & 6 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 3 & 2 & 1 \\
\cline{4-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 9 & 0 \\
\end{array}
$$
We see that the digits after the decimal point repeat after the 5th digit.
Therefore, the first nonzero digit to the right of the decimal point is $\boxed{7}$.
The answer is: 7

To convert $8/11$ to a decimal, we perform long division. The remainder after each division is the numerator of the next division, so we have\[
\begin{array}{c|cccccccccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0.} & 7 & 2 & 7 & 2 & 7 & 2 & \ldots \\
\cline{2-11}
11 & 8 & . & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & \ldots \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{8} & & 8 & & 8 & & 8 & \\
\cline{2-4}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 2 & 0 & & & & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 1 & 1 & & & & & \\
\cline{3-5}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 8 & 0 & & & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & 7 & 7 & & & & \\
\cline{4-6}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 3 & 0 & & & \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & & & 2 & 7 & & & \\
\cline{5-7}
\end{array}
\]Since we have a remainder of 3 after the first division, and a remainder of 30 after the second division, the repeating block starts after two decimal places.
Thus, the least number of digits in a repeating block is $\boxed{2}$.
The answer is: 2

